I see now. Evidently it *had been* the intent of at least the person who 
wrote the quote for the lower-level spell to be available to the caster. 
It's odd that the comment described an effect that did not obtain, rather 
than describe what "should" happen (but hadn't been coded yet).

My guess, based simply on the two comments, would be that two issues were 
anticipated by the coders. One is an interface issue: each time a particular 
type of spell was cast, the game would prompt the player to choose either 
the higher or lower level spell. The other involves the cost of the spell: 
if the higher version were chosen, more mana and hunger points would be 
expended. Since it was decided that the increased effect would obtain 
"without additional cost to the spellcaster" (4.4.1 comment), it may have 
been thought that no one have a reason to choose the lower level spell; 
thus, the need for continual interface prompts wouldn't be necessary.

Unfortunately, all this doesn't directly address the differences between RAY 
and DISTANT types of spellcasting. (The comment seems to address all spell 
effects, although it does come immediately before cone of cold and fireball 
code). The interface issue is why I suggested that targetting a square 
immediately adjacent to the hero would be equivalent to a directional 
command and invoke the RAY effect: no need for a special prompt. Why else 
would you target such a square? If you want to cast the spell on yourself, 
you could still choose "." as your direction; if a monster is immediately 
adjacent to you, the ray would still terminate on that square and explode.
